{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01V4GsgUCte5kZ6DcbnE6aY6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a very good picture. Let me now look at the packet_loop.rs to understand the LoopParam and related structures.\n\nKey findings:\n1. `SpinbitVersion` has variants: Basic=0, Random=1, Null=2, On=3\n2. `ServerSockets<S: Socket>` exists in socks.rs\n3. `PacketLoopCbFn` trait exists in packet_loop.rs\n4. `LoopParam` struct exists in packet_loop.rs\n\n\n5. `ServerSockets::send_through` and a `select` free function are also available\n\nI have enough context now to start writing the files. For satellite.rs, I can follow the high_latency.rs pattern closely, but I'll need to add congestion algorithm configuration on the client connection, adjust the preemptive repeat settings, set up binlog, and handle a more complex post-processing step.\n\nLooking at the C code mappings, I can see that `set_preemptive_repeat_policy` on the server and `set_preemptive_repeat` on the client connection are already implemented in the Rust bindings, along with `set_pmtud_required` which the satellite test also calls.\n\nFor the flow control checks, I need to add a few accessor methods: one to get the server's `nb_data_nodes_allocated_max` field and another to retrieve the send MTU for a specific path on a connection. I also need to figure out whether `binlog_new_connection` is a method or a standalone function based on the binlog.rs definitions.\n\nLooking at the socket test requirements, I'm mapping out the C FFI calls to Rust equivalents—things like opening and closing server sockets, setting ECN options, and handling socket selection operations.\n\nThe test itself is a real network integration test that opens server sockets on a specific port, then runs ping-pong exchanges with different addresses (127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost) to verify the sockets work correctly, plus a separate test for ECN option handling.\n\nFor the Rust version, I need to leverage the existing `ServerSockets<Socket2Udp>` API, but the tricky part is that the C test also sends raw packets directly from the client side using `sendto()` rather than going through picoquic's socket abstraction. I'll need to structure the test functions similarly—resolving addresses, performing ping-pong exchanges with raw socket operations, and testing across multiple ports and address families.\n\nNow let me check what the `ServerSockets` implementation looks like to understand what I'm working with.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3484,"cache_read_input_tokens":157544,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3484},"output_tokens":35,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb85c5eb-b826-43b9-84a4-4be9b46756f2","uuid":"db8046ac-d39a-4692-bca5-9400d1dc59a3"}
