{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SnRg1rdgri2nFYYXKfn5C3","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `picoquic/picosocks.h` plus the Phase 2 socket\n2\t//! abstraction.\n3\t//!\n4\t//! Phase 2 reshape: the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a Linux file descriptor)\n5\t//! is replaced by a [`Socket`] *trait* that the host platform\n6\t//! implements.  The default implementation wraps\n7\t//! [`socket2::Socket`] (the canonical cross-platform UDP socket\n8\t//! crate); other backends (no-std embedded, Windows-specific,\n9\t//! the test simulator) implement the same trait.\n10\t//! [`ServerSockets<S>`] is generic over the implementor.\n11\t//!\n12\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every method body is\n13\t//! `todo!()`.\n14\t\n15\tuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n16\t\n17\tuse crate::Error;\n18\tuse crate::Instant;\n19\t\n20\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n21\t// Constants and supporting types.\n22\t\n23\t/// Number of UDP sockets a server context owns — one IPv4, one\n24\t/// IPv6.  C: `PICOQUIC_NB_SERVER_SOCKETS`.\n25\tpub const SERVER_SOCKET_COUNT: usize = 2;\n26\t\n27\t/// `MessageHeader` is a re-export of [`socket2::MsgHdr`] — the\n28\t/// canonical cross-platform `msghdr` wrapper.  Phase 4 fills in\n29\t/// the bodies that thread one through the cmsg helpers.\n30\tpub type MessageHeader<'addr, 'bufs, 'control> = socket2::MsgHdr<'addr, 'bufs, 'control>;\n31\t\n32\t/// OS error code surfaced by socket sends when the underlying\n33\t/// syscall fails.  Wraps the raw Linux `errno` (or its Windows\n34\t/// equivalent on other targets) and exposes\n35\t/// [`OsError::is_unreachable`] for the path-abandon decision the\n36\t/// C `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable` helper made.\n37\t#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\n38\tpub struct OsError(pub i32);\n39\t\n40\timpl OsError {\n41\t    /// Whether this errno implies the destination is unreachable\n42\t    /// and the owning path should be abandoned.  C:\n43\t    /// `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable`.\n44\t    pub fn is_unreachable(self) -> bool {\n45\t        todo!()\n46\t    }\n47\t}\n48\t\n49\t// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n50\t// `Socket` trait.\n51\t\n52\t/// One UDP socket on the application's network stack.  Replaces\n53\t/// the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a raw file descriptor); the host\n54\t/// implementation supplies open / bind / send / recv operations\n55\t/// while the rest of picoquic stays platform-agnostic.\n56\t///\n57\t/// The default [`socket2::Socket`] implementation is in\n58\t/// [`crate::socks_socket2`] (gated on the `std` feature).\n59\tpub trait Socket {\n60\t    /// Local address bound to this socket (`getsockname`).\n61\t    /// C: `picoquic_get_local_address`.\n62\t    fn local_address(&self) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error>;\n63\t\n64\t    /// Receive one UDP datagram into `buffer`.  Output parameters\n65\t    /// (source / dest address, interface index, ECN, byte count)\n66\t    /// fold into [`RecvInfo`]; the slice length subsumes the C\n67\t    /// `buffer_max` parameter.  C: `picoquic_recvmsg`.\n68\t    fn recv(&mut self, buffer: &mut [u8]) -> Result<RecvInfo, Error>;\n69\t\n70\t    /// Send `bytes` to `addr_dest` from `addr_from` with an\n71\t    /// optional GSO segmentation hint `gso_size` (`UDP_SEGMENT`).\n72\t    /// `addr_from = None` lets the kernel choose the source\n73\t    /// address.  `Ok(n)` reports bytes sent; `Err` carries the\n74\t    /// OS error.  C: `picoquic_sendmsg`.\n75\t    fn send(\n76\t        &mut self,\n77\t        addr_dest: &SocketAddr,\n78\t        addr_from: Option<&SocketAddr>,\n79\t        dest_if: i32,\n80\t        bytes: &[u8],\n81\t        gso_size: i32,\n82\t    ) -> Result<usize, OsError>;\n83\t\n84\t    /// Enable per-packet destination-info delivery on this\n85\t    /// socket (`IP_PKTINFO` / `IPV6_RECVPKTINFO`).  Default:\n86\t    /// no-op for backends that don't surface pktinfo.\n87\t    fn set_pkt_info(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n88\t        Ok(())\n89\t    }\n90\t\n91\t    /// Enable ECN reception (and request `ECN_ECT_1` outbound).\n92\t    /// Returns `(recv_set, send_set)` indicating whether the\n93\t    /// kernel honored each socket option.  Default: no-op,\n94\t    /// returns `(false, false)`.\n95\t    fn set_ecn_options(&mut self) -> Result<(bool, bool), Error> {\n96\t        Ok((false, false))\n97\t    }\n98\t\n99\t    /// Like [`Self::set_ecn_options`] but with an explicit\n100\t    /// outbound TOS / ECN value (`ecn` is one of the 2-bit"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb85c5eb-b826-43b9-84a4-4be9b46756f2","uuid":"1850fc05-6c2d-4eb4-9e7b-3de0f0bbc89c","timestamp":"2026-05-05T11:54:32.002Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/socks.rs","content":"//! Translation of `picoquic/picosocks.h` plus the Phase 2 socket\n//! abstraction.\n//!\n//! Phase 2 reshape: the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a Linux file descriptor)\n//! is replaced by a [`Socket`] *trait* that the host platform\n//! implements.  The default implementation wraps\n//! [`socket2::Socket`] (the canonical cross-platform UDP socket\n//! crate); other backends (no-std embedded, Windows-specific,\n//! the test simulator) implement the same trait.\n//! [`ServerSockets<S>`] is generic over the implementor.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every method body is\n//! `todo!()`.\n\nuse core::net::SocketAddr;\n\nuse crate::Error;\nuse crate::Instant;\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// Constants and supporting types.\n\n/// Number of UDP sockets a server context owns — one IPv4, one\n/// IPv6.  C: `PICOQUIC_NB_SERVER_SOCKETS`.\npub const SERVER_SOCKET_COUNT: usize = 2;\n\n/// `MessageHeader` is a re-export of [`socket2::MsgHdr`] — the\n/// canonical cross-platform `msghdr` wrapper.  Phase 4 fills in\n/// the bodies that thread one through the cmsg helpers.\npub type MessageHeader<'addr, 'bufs, 'control> = socket2::MsgHdr<'addr, 'bufs, 'control>;\n\n/// OS error code surfaced by socket sends when the underlying\n/// syscall fails.  Wraps the raw Linux `errno` (or its Windows\n/// equivalent on other targets) and exposes\n/// [`OsError::is_unreachable`] for the path-abandon decision the\n/// C `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable` helper made.\n#[derive(Debug, Copy, Clone, PartialEq, Eq)]\npub struct OsError(pub i32);\n\nimpl OsError {\n    /// Whether this errno implies the destination is unreachable\n    /// and the owning path should be abandoned.  C:\n    /// `picoquic_socket_error_implies_unreachable`.\n    pub fn is_unreachable(self) -> bool {\n        todo!()\n    }\n}\n\n// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n// `Socket` trait.\n\n/// One UDP socket on the application's network stack.  Replaces\n/// the C `SOCKET_TYPE` (a raw file descriptor); the host\n/// implementation supplies open / bind / send / recv operations\n/// while the rest of picoquic stays platform-agnostic.\n///\n/// The default [`socket2::Socket`] implementation is in\n/// [`crate::socks_socket2`] (gated on the `std` feature).\npub trait Socket {\n    /// Local address bound to this socket (`getsockname`).\n    /// C: `picoquic_get_local_address`.\n    fn local_address(&self) -> Result<SocketAddr, Error>;\n\n    /// Receive one UDP datagram into `buffer`.  Output parameters\n    /// (source / dest address, interface index, ECN, byte count)\n    /// fold into [`RecvInfo`]; the slice length subsumes the C\n    /// `buffer_max` parameter.  C: `picoquic_recvmsg`.\n    fn recv(&mut self, buffer: &mut [u8]) -> Result<RecvInfo, Error>;\n\n    /// Send `bytes` to `addr_dest` from `addr_from` with an\n    /// optional GSO segmentation hint `gso_size` (`UDP_SEGMENT`).\n    /// `addr_from = None` lets the kernel choose the source\n    /// address.  `Ok(n)` reports bytes sent; `Err` carries the\n    /// OS error.  C: `picoquic_sendmsg`.\n    fn send(\n        &mut self,\n        addr_dest: &SocketAddr,\n        addr_from: Option<&SocketAddr>,\n        dest_if: i32,\n        bytes: &[u8],\n        gso_size: i32,\n    ) -> Result<usize, OsError>;\n\n    /// Enable per-packet destination-info delivery on this\n    /// socket (`IP_PKTINFO` / `IPV6_RECVPKTINFO`).  Default:\n    /// no-op for backends that don't surface pktinfo.\n    fn set_pkt_info(&mut self) -> Result<(), Error> {\n        Ok(())\n    }\n\n    /// Enable ECN reception (and request `ECN_ECT_1` outbound).\n    /// Returns `(recv_set, send_set)` indicating whether the\n    /// kernel honored each socket option.  Default: no-op,\n    /// returns `(false, false)`.\n    fn set_ecn_options(&mut self) -> Result<(bool, bool), Error> {\n        Ok((false, false))\n    }\n\n    /// Like [`Self::set_ecn_options`] but with an explicit\n    /// outbound TOS / ECN value (`ecn` is one of the 2-bit","numLines":100,"startLine":1,"totalLines":303}}}
