{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016EurYndjTtd42TQV9b7b4n","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/packet_loop.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Bundle of types and entry points implementing quic's\n4\t//! reference event loop (`sockloop.c`) on top of the [`socks`]\n5\t//! UDP shims.  The loop:\n6\t//!\n7\t//! * owns one or more [`SocketCtx`] per network family,\n8\t//! * polls them with [`socks::select`] (or `select`/\n9\t//!   `WSAWaitForMultipleEvents` in the C original),\n10\t//! * routes received datagrams into quic, and\n11\t//! * fires application callbacks ([`PacketLoopCbFn`]) at\n12\t//!   well-defined points (loop ready, after recv, after send,\n13\t//!   address change, time check, system-call duration spike, wake-up,\n14\t//!   alt-port).\n15\t//!\n16\t//! Three layered entry points cover the same loop with different\n17\t//! parameter shapes:\n18\t//!\n19\t//! * [`run`] — the original 1.x signature, preserved for\n20\t//!   compatibility.  Builds a [`LoopParam`] on the stack and forwards.\n21\t//! * [`run_v2`] — takes a [`LoopParam`] directly.\n22\t//! * [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] — takes a fully-populated\n23\t//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`]; the threaded entry point.\n24\t//!\n25\t//! Plus the threading helpers ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`] et al.)\n26\t//! that wrap `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n27\t//!\n28\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every function body is\n29\t//! `todo!()` and the empty `#[cfg(test)] mod test {}` lands at the\n30\t//! bottom for Phase 2 to fill.\n31\t//!\n32\t//! [`socks`]: crate::socks\n33\t//!\n34\t//! ## Pointer-shape decisions\n35\t//!\n36\t//! Read from `quic/sockloop.c`, `quic/winsockloop.c`, and\n37\t//! the demo callers in `first/demo.c`,\n38\t//! `sample/sample_*.c`, and `test/sockloop_test.c`:\n39\t//!\n40\t//! * `Quic*` parameters are non-null in every observed\n41\t//!   caller — they translate to `&mut Quic`.\n42\t//! * `LoopParam*` is *borrowed* by the loop\n43\t//!   helpers (the caller stack-allocates it in\n44\t//!   `run_v2` / the demo apps) — `&mut` here.  Inside\n45\t//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`] the same pointer is *owned*\n46\t//!   when `is_param_allocated == 1` ([`Config::start_server_threads`]\n47\t//!   `malloc`s a copy and `delete_network_thread` `free`s\n48\t//!   it).  Phase 1 keeps the field as `Option<Box<…>>` and folds\n49\t//!   the C `is_param_allocated` flag away — the `Box` itself\n50\t//!   carries ownership; borrowed-param call sites store `None`\n51\t//!   plus a separate borrow at runtime.\n52\t//! * `picoquic_quic_config_t*` is borrowed-mut by\n53\t//!   [`Quic::create_server`] and [`Config::start_server_threads`]\n54\t//!   (they read and may mutate fields like `ticket_encryption_key`).\n55\t//! * Function-pointer typedefs (`packet_loop_cb_fn`,\n56\t//!   `custom_thread_create_fn`, `_setname_fn`,\n57\t//!   `_delete_fn`) become traits per the Phase 1 rules; the C `void*\n58\t//!   callback_ctx` companion folds into the trait implementor's\n59\t//!   state.  The C `(cb_mode, void* callback_argv)` tagged-union\n60\t//!   pair becomes the typed [`LoopEvent`] enum — each variant\n61\t//!   carries the payload type the C `cb_mode` implied.\n62\t//! * `SocketCtx* s_ctx` is used as both a single object\n63\t//!   ([`SocketCtx::close`]) and a fixed-size array\n64\t//!   ([`open_sockets`] writes up to `PACKET_LOOP_SOCKETS_MAX`\n65\t//!   entries).  The single-object path is a method on [`SocketCtx`];\n66\t//!   the array path takes `&mut [SocketCtx]`, with the slice length\n67\t//!   subsuming the C convention of \"callee writes and returns the count\".\n68\t//! * `NetworkThreadCtx**` outputs from\n69\t//!   [`Config::start_server_threads`] become `&mut [Option<Box<…>>]`\n70\t//!   — the slice carries `nb_threads_max`; each slot is filled with\n71\t//!   `Some` on success.\n72\t//! * Bitfields collapse to `bool`s.  Single-bit C `int : 1` /\n73\t//!   `unsigned int : 1` flag fields don't justify `bitflags!` on a\n74\t//!   set of unrelated booleans (see `config.rs` for the\n75\t//!   established convention).\n76\t//! * `volatile int` fields in [`NetworkThreadCtx`]\n77\t//!   become plain `i32` / `bool` — `Send`/`Sync` is out of v1 scope,\n78\t//!   so the volatile semantics have nowhere to land.\n79\t//! * `sockaddr_storage` fields fold into `Option<SocketAddr>`,\n80\t//!   matching the [`socks`] convention (`AF_UNSPEC` ↔ `None`)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb85c5eb-b826-43b9-84a4-4be9b46756f2","uuid":"eb088679-4ec8-4679-b3fd-68937775c9e1","timestamp":"2026-05-05T11:55:09.592Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/packet_loop.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/packet_loop.h`.\n//!\n//! Bundle of types and entry points implementing quic's\n//! reference event loop (`sockloop.c`) on top of the [`socks`]\n//! UDP shims.  The loop:\n//!\n//! * owns one or more [`SocketCtx`] per network family,\n//! * polls them with [`socks::select`] (or `select`/\n//!   `WSAWaitForMultipleEvents` in the C original),\n//! * routes received datagrams into quic, and\n//! * fires application callbacks ([`PacketLoopCbFn`]) at\n//!   well-defined points (loop ready, after recv, after send,\n//!   address change, time check, system-call duration spike, wake-up,\n//!   alt-port).\n//!\n//! Three layered entry points cover the same loop with different\n//! parameter shapes:\n//!\n//! * [`run`] — the original 1.x signature, preserved for\n//!   compatibility.  Builds a [`LoopParam`] on the stack and forwards.\n//! * [`run_v2`] — takes a [`LoopParam`] directly.\n//! * [`NetworkThreadCtx::run`] — takes a fully-populated\n//!   [`NetworkThreadCtx`]; the threaded entry point.\n//!\n//! Plus the threading helpers ([`NetworkThreadCtx::spawn`] et al.)\n//! that wrap `pthread_create` / `CreateThread`.\n//!\n//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every function body is\n//! `todo!()` and the empty `#[cfg(test)] mod test {}`