ore complex random and socket tests. Writing the test files...\n\nThe satellite test needs to set up a bunch of transport and congestion parameters—initial CID bytes with algorithm and network metrics, flow control limits, TLS initialization, congestion algorithm configuration on both sides, link parameters, bandwidth seeding, PMTUD settings, and binlog configuration—then call the main test body with the data size.\n\nFor the Rust side, I need to figure out how to pass `data_size` when the current function signature has `_cwin_blocked` as an i32 parameter instead. Looking at the C mapping, I need to understand what parameters correspond to what. I'm realizing I need to look at the actual C function signature to understand what parameters map to what. Looking back at a previous test translation, I can see how the arguments were passed, and comparing that to the C code shows me that `_cwin_blocked` shouldn't receive the data size—it should just be 0 since it's marked as unused anyway. I'm looking at the Rust function signatures from congestion.rs to understand the parameter mapping. The Rust version takes the scenario, loss mask, and a blocked window size parameter, along with timing values for simulation and completion targets.\n\nComparing the C and Rust calls for the satellite test, I notice the data_size parameter gets cast to i32 in Rust, and while 100MB fits within the i32 range, I need to verify the exact parameter positions and types match up correctly between the two implementations. Looking at the satellite test function, the data size is 100MB which safely fits in an i32, and I'm setting up the Rust equivalent by calling the test scenario body with the appropriate parameters including the data size and completion time values.\n\nNow I need to figure out how to access the congestion algorithm number in Rust since the struct approach differs from the C implementation where it's directly accessed from the algorithm pointer.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null