{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SBgFRnXyaotwdhqBSzfLwC","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Translation of `quic/binlog.h`.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! Public surface of the binary trace (\"binlog\") backend.  The\n4\t//! header exposes two flavours of entry point:\n5\t//!\n6\t//! 1. **Top-level wiring** ([`Quic::set_binlog`],\n7\t//!    [`Quic::enable_binlog`]) that installs the binlog vtable on a\n8\t//!    QUIC context.  `binlog_dir == NULL` in C is the \"stop tracing\"\n9\t//!    sentinel; [`Quic::enable_binlog`] only flips the vtable\n10\t//!    pointer and is used when autoqlog wants the binary stream\n11\t//!    without a dedicated directory.\n12\t//! 2. **Per-event writers** that emit one trace record.  In C they\n13\t//!    all bottom out in `fwrite()` against either an out-of-band\n14\t//!    `FILE*` or `connection->f_binlog` pulled from the connection.  The\n15\t//!    Rust split mirrors that: the three file-only writers ([`pdu`],\n16\t//!    [`packet`], [`tls_ticket`]) are free functions on a [`File`]\n17\t//!    sink, and the rest hang on the [`Binlog`] trait implemented for\n18\t//!    [`Connection`] (they pull the file handle from\n19\t//!    `connection.f_binlog` themselves).\n20\t//!\n21\t//! Phase 1 contract: signatures only — every body is `todo!()`.\n22\t//! Bodies and the empty-test module land in later phases.\n23\t//!\n24\t//! Pointer-shape and translation policy notes for this module:\n25\t//!\n26\t//! * `FILE*` (the three low-level writers) → `&mut std::fs::File`.\n27\t//!   These calls borrow the handle for the duration of one record\n28\t//!   write — ownership stays with the connection (`connection->f_binlog`)\n29\t//!   or the caller.  The binlog stream is *binary*, so the text-side\n30\t//!   `&mut impl core::fmt::Write` convention used by\n31\t//!   [`crate::textlog`] does not apply here.\n32\t//! * `Quic*` / `Connection*` — every observed caller passes a non-NULL\n33\t//!   handle and the body mutates internal state (`quic->bin_log_fns`,\n34\t//!   `connection->f_binlog`, `quic->binlog_dir`).  Free functions\n35\t//!   keyed on `Quic*` become inherent methods on [`Quic`]; those keyed\n36\t//!   on `Connection*` become methods on the [`Binlog`] trait\n37\t//!   (implemented for [`Connection`]).\n38\t//! * `Path*` — only ever accessed inside\n39\t//!   `binlog_get_path_id(connection, path_x)`, which dereferences `path_x`\n40\t//!   to read `unique_path_id`.  Every observed caller passes a\n41\t//!   non-NULL path handle, so this is `&mut Path` for parity with\n42\t//!   the unified-log dispatch trait (which takes the path mutably\n43\t//!   for the same hooks).\n44\t//! * `const ConnectionId*` (in [`pdu`] / [`packet`]) →\n45\t//!   `&ConnectionId`.  The C contract is \"must be non-NULL\"; every\n46\t//!   caller passes `&connection->initial_connection_id`.\n47\t//! * `ConnectionId* dcid` (in [`Binlog::packet_lost`]) is\n48\t//!   nullable per `loss_recovery.c` — when no remote CID is known\n49\t//!   the C call site passes NULL and the body emits a single zero\n50\t//!   length byte.  Maps to `Option<&ConnectionId>`.  The C signature\n51\t//!   drops `const` but the body only reads through the pointer, so\n52\t//!   the Rust equivalent stays a shared borrow.\n53\t//! * `packet_header* ph` (in [`Binlog::dropped_packet`],\n54\t//!   [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`]) — the body only *reads*\n55\t//!   `ph->ptype` in the dropped path and reconstructs a fresh header\n56\t//!   in the outgoing path.  In [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`] the\n57\t//!   parsed-header buffer is constructed locally, so no pointer\n58\t//!   crosses the API boundary.  In [`packet`] /\n59\t//!   [`Binlog::dropped_packet`] we map `ph` to `&PacketHeader`\n60\t//!   (immutable borrow) — matching the unified-log trait shape.\n61\t//! * `ConnectionId connection_id` (in [`tls_ticket`]) is `Copy` and\n62\t//!   pass-by-value, mirroring the C ABI.\n63\t//! * `const struct sockaddr*` pairs (`addr_peer`, `addr_local` in\n64\t//!   [`pdu`]) → `&core::net::SocketAddr`, matching the convention\n65\t//!   established in [`crate::logger`].\n66\t//! * `const uint8_t* + size_t` argument pairs collapse to `&[u8]`\n67\t//!   (`bytes`/`bytes_max` in [`packet`], `params`/`param_length` in\n68\t//!   [`Binlog::transport_extension`], `ticket`/`ticket_length`\n69\t//!   in [`tls_ticket`], `sni`/`sni_len` and `alpn`/`alpn_len` in\n70\t//!   [`Binlog::negotiated_alpn`]).  An empty slice models the C\n71\t//!   `(NULL, 0)` callers cleanly.\n72\t//! * [`Binlog::outgoing_packet`] carries *two* buffers: the\n73\t//!   unencrypted `bytes` (length passed separately as `length`) and\n74\t//!   the encrypted `send_buffer` (length passed separately as\n75\t//!   `send_length`).  Each pair collapses to one `&[u8]`.  The\n76\t//!   `pn_length` parameter — the offset of the packet-number field\n77\t//!   inside `bytes` — survives as a separate `usize`.\n78\t//! * `const PtlsIovec* alpn_list, size_t alpn_count` →\n79\t//!   `&[&[u8]]`, mirroring the unified-log trait shape.\n80\t//! * `int receiving` / `int is_local` are pure 0/1 flags promoted to"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"cb85c5eb-b826-43b9-84a4-4be9b46756f2","uuid":"b50702d0-a2e6-4ad4-a955-702cd21bb9ec","timestamp":"2026-05-05T11:56:51.057Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/binlog.rs","content":"//! Translation of `quic/binlog.h`.\n//!\n//! Public surface of the binary trace (\"binlog\") backend.  The\n//! header exposes two flavours of entry point:\n//!\n//! 1. **Top-level wiring** ([`Quic::set_binlog`],\n//!    [`Quic::enable_binlog`]) that installs the binlog vtable on a\n//!    QUIC context.  `binlog_dir == NULL` in C is the \"stop tracing\"\n//!    sentinel; [`Quic::enable_binlog`] only flips the vtable\n//!    pointer and is used when autoqlog wants the binary stream\n//!    without a dedicated directory.\n//! 2. **Per-event writers** that emit one trace record.  